\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{From the half-turn to a finite group}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{For the four-cycle half-turn, #{G=C_2=\{1,r\}} and
#{\rho(r)=P}. Its two [projectors](fdip-003O) are
#{(\bbI+P)/2} and #{(\bbI-P)/2}, with the concrete two-dimensional
[ranges](fdip-000L). A larger symmetry group can split these ranges
further; a sector for one chosen group is not an intrinsic label
independent of that choice.}

\p{The [group-algebra extension](fdip-003N) gives
##{\widehat\rho:\mathbb R[G]\longrightarrow\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(H),
\qquad \widehat\rho\left(\sum_g a_g[g]\right)=\sum_g a_g\rho(g).}
It uses the [group algebra](fgap-001H) and
[representation/module correspondence](fgap-001I).
Since every #{\rho(g)} commutes with #{Q}, so does every
#{\widehat\rho(a)}. The [action groupoid](fdip-000X)
organizes transported backgrounds, whereas this representation describes
a fixed background's symmetry. The character-projector construction
in \citet{Section 2.6, Theorem 8, pp. 21-22}{serre1977linear}
recovers exactly the two half-turn projectors.}
